{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789841600,"nonce":"QkjILwezYpbGtF4xFH7jOpTM2/c=","message":"9885ba3: the guard-hoisting pass (src/mir/opt/hoist_guards.rs) is in and works — in advance() the guard on a moves from the inner loop to bb2 after the slow-path exit at pc 169, and the field-kind invariant still applies because the LLVM class facts now follow a receiver through copies and loop-carried parameters to the entered value. But on the NUC (x86-64) it makes ash/nbody 5% slower, 0.80 → 0.85: the inner loops are the same length, and the difference is register pressure — with the guard gone, LLVM's allocator spills the loop bound to the stack and rematerialises the class constant each iteration (cmpq %r10,-128(%rbp); movabsq …). On aarch64 the A/B under a loaded Mac was a wash. Left off by default behind WLIFT_HOIST_GUARDS=1. The remaining inner-loop work in advance() is inherent to the dynamic checks: b's class check, its field loads, and the elements-pointer reload behind the list type test.","files":null}]}